# deploy.conf — host-specific deploy values (the ONLY place a host differs).
#
# Copy to deploy/deploy.conf (gitignored, like the /etc/zfin-data-api env file) and fill in for
# THIS host, then run deploy/render-config.sh to generate the systemd units + Caddy site file from
# the committed *.tmpl files. Nothing host-specific is baked into the tracked files, so porting to
# another droplet is: edit these values, re-render.
#
# The values below are do2's intended values, as a working reference.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# TWO ACCOUNTS, because there are two jobs
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# DEPLOY identity. Owns the checkout, runs git pull / npm ci / npm run build, and needs sudo —
# installing systemd units and reloading Caddy is root's work. The `-admin` suffix follows the
# convention already on this droplet (dg-tour-admin). This is the account you log into to operate
# the box, and the facility servers will share it when they follow.
#
# It needs a real password: sudo authenticates against the password field, so an account created
# with --disabled-password is in the sudo group and still cannot sudo.
SERVICE_USER=tsw-admin

# RUNTIME identity. What the API and loader processes actually run as. A system account: no
# password, no shell, no sudo, no home. It needs exactly four things — read the checkout, read
# /etc/zfin-data-api/zfin-data-api.env, listen on APP_PORT, reach MySQL — and a flaw in the Node
# process yields exactly those and nothing else.
#
# One runtime account PER SERVICE, not one shared across everything on the box. That is the same
# reasoning as the per-facility database users: a compromise should reach one service's credentials,
# not every service's. When the facility servers land here they each get their own, all sharing a
# group that can read the one shared checkout.
RUNTIME_USER=tsw

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The code checkout root — also the systemd WorkingDirectory for both units.
#
# /srv, NOT a home directory. The runtime account has to read this tree, and a home directory is
# 0750 to its owner — `tsw` simply cannot traverse into /home/tsw-admin. That alone settles it; the
# units additionally set ProtectHome=yes, which hides /home from them altogether.
#
# Owned by the deploy user, group-readable by the runtime user, setgid so that a fresh `npm ci`
# keeps the group on everything it writes:
#     sudo install -d -o tsw-admin -g tsw -m 2750 /srv/zfin-data-api
APP_DIR=/srv/zfin-data-api

# Absolute path to the Node binary (systemd has no nvm or PATH). Node 20+ is required by NestJS 11.
#
# A SYSTEM Node, not one under ~/.nvm. Same reason as APP_DIR: the runtime account cannot read
# another user's home, and ProtectHome=yes hides /home from the unit besides. Installing it
# system-wide also means the version that builds the code is the version that runs it. do1 and
# dg-tour both carry an absolute nvm path in ExecStart; this drops that wart rather than
# reproducing it.
NODE_BIN=/usr/bin/node

# The public site hostname Caddy serves and auto-provisions a Let's Encrypt cert for. DNS must
# already resolve this name to the droplet before the Caddy step.
#
# FOR A STAGED CUTOVER, point a temporary name here first (e.g. zfin2.zebrafishfacilitymanager.com),
# verify against the live do1 service, then re-render with the real name once DNS moves.
SITE_ADDRESS=zfin.zebrafishfacilitymanager.com

# The localhost port the API listens on. Must match PORT in /etc/zfin-data-api/zfin-data-api.env.
# do2 already runs dg-tour on 3478 (api) and 3479 (ingestor).
APP_PORT=3480
